WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:02.169
- Moving very quickly on.

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:03.703
The eagle-eyed amongst you all

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:07.040
notice that we have 
two chairs next to me.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.577
One of these chairs 
is for Kerry Flynn

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:13.814
who is a journalist who's
written for Huffington Post,

00:00:13.814 --> 00:00:17.184
Forbes, Money Magazine,
and is now business reporter

00:00:17.184 --> 00:00:21.054
for Mashable with a particular
specialism in the tech industry.

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:23.924
The other chair is 
for Gary Vaynerchuk

00:00:23.924 --> 00:00:27.361
who has a formidable 
headline which is

00:00:27.361 --> 00:00:30.197
Gary Vaynerchuk 
Builds Businesses.

00:00:30.197 --> 00:00:33.800
And that is something that
absolutely no one can argue.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.903
Straight out of college he
took his family business

00:00:35.903 --> 00:00:39.806
from three million to 
60 million in five years.

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:43.243
He's a prolific angel
investor and venture capitalist

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:46.546
with investments including
Uber, Facebook, and BirchBox.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:48.448
He now runs VaynerMedia,

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:51.318
social media 
first digital agency

00:00:51.318 --> 00:00:54.821
and has been named to
both Fortune and Craine's

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:57.324
40 under 40 lists this year.

00:00:57.324 --> 00:00:59.293
Please welcome Carrie and Gary.

00:00:59.293 --> 00:01:01.528
(applause)

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.901
- Hello.

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:08.769
- Hey, guys.

00:01:08.769 --> 00:01:11.371
Thank you for that 
great introduction.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:13.340
Yes, I'm Kerry Flynn, 
I write for Mashable.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:14.908
I'm here with Gary.

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:17.844
You got a brief introduction,
I'm thinking you guys

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.680
probably all know of 
Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.914
but I would love, Gary,

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:22.950
for you to just 
introduce yourself.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.820
When you go to an event, 
how do you introduce yourself?

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.790
- Well, I think the
fragmentation of attention

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:33.126
and marketing and 
coms is so intriguing.

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:35.162
How many people here
have no idea who I am?

00:01:35.162 --> 00:01:36.496
Raise your hand.

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:38.365
Oh, that was better.

00:01:38.365 --> 00:01:39.800
That was a good ego boost.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.335
(laughter)

00:01:41.335 --> 00:01:44.338
Usually how I go in to
conferences is ask that question

00:01:44.338 --> 00:01:46.540
and the number is 
always very, very high,

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.809
and I can always see
the organizers squirming

00:01:48.809 --> 00:01:51.778
of either they overpaid me
or they feel bad for me.

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:52.980
(laughter)

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.150
But usually, I contextualize
myself as an entrepreneur.

00:01:58.251 --> 00:02:00.754
But I always reverse
engineer the audience.

00:02:00.754 --> 00:02:05.826
My biggest want up here in
our next 30 minutes is...

00:02:05.826 --> 00:02:08.228
I was walking around and
looking at name tags,

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:09.763
asked ahead of time, we talked.

00:02:09.763 --> 00:02:12.633
To me, what I'm gonna
deliver is the context

00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:14.501
of what I think is the
most interesting and most

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:17.671
valuable thing to talk about
to the people in the room.

00:02:17.671 --> 00:02:20.240
I think as somebody who's
been on stage a lot,

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.845
I'm fascinated by how 
80% of presentations

00:02:24.845 --> 00:02:27.514
are just one long press release.

00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:30.183
I think I take 
this very seriously,

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:33.754
and the way I introduce myself
usually is under the context

00:02:33.754 --> 00:02:35.756
of what could 
conceivably bring value

00:02:35.756 --> 00:02:37.491
to the people in the audience.

00:02:37.491 --> 00:02:38.325
- Great.

00:02:38.325 --> 00:02:40.761
Well, then let's talk a
little bit about VaynerMedia

00:02:40.761 --> 00:02:42.929
in terms of building that
out because it seems to be

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:45.298
you really went with 
that mission in mind.

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:48.235
What did you really
want to offer the world

00:02:48.235 --> 00:02:50.337
that didn't exist before?

00:02:52.406 --> 00:02:54.441
- I grew up in my family's
liquor store business

00:02:54.441 --> 00:02:55.509
and I launched one of the first

00:02:55.509 --> 00:02:57.978
e-commerce wine 
businesses in 1996.

00:02:57.978 --> 00:03:00.180
And I built my 
business with no money,

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.949
and the reason it became
a big business was because

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:04.751
I bought Google Adwords
the day it came out

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:07.487
for five cents a 
click for the word wine,

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:10.490
and because 
I started a YouTube show

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.426
five months after 
YouTube came out,

00:03:12.426 --> 00:03:16.396
and because I had an
email newsletter in 1997

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:19.032
that had 91% open rates.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:20.300
(laughter)

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.869
So, my entire narrative
has been very simple.

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:24.371
And I think for all 
of us in this room,

00:03:24.371 --> 00:03:26.473
I think the 
world is pretty basic,

00:03:26.473 --> 00:03:28.608
mainly because I'm pretty
basic, which is there's

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:31.812
overpriced attention and
there's under-priced attention.

00:03:31.812 --> 00:03:35.082
And while most of the
world spends a lot of time

00:03:35.082 --> 00:03:38.618
trying to quantify 
and find the R.O.I.

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:41.655
of under-priced attention,
I think the better model

00:03:41.655 --> 00:03:45.292
is to be a practitioner
on your intuition around

00:03:45.292 --> 00:03:48.395
normal people's behavior, 
learn it,

00:03:48.395 --> 00:03:49.863
and maximize the R.O.I.

00:03:49.863 --> 00:03:52.732
So, I did that for my family
business, that went great.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.067
Then I invested in 
all these companies:

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.703
Facebook, Twitter, Tumblr,
made a lot of money,

00:03:56.703 --> 00:03:59.439
and then I decided, in my
continued mission to buy

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:02.509
the New York Jets, 
though they're testing me,

00:04:02.509 --> 00:04:04.044
(laughter)

00:04:04.044 --> 00:04:05.712
that the way I was 
gonna do that was

00:04:05.712 --> 00:04:07.781
to buy brands and flip them.

00:04:07.781 --> 00:04:09.516
So, I think nostalgia
is the most under-priced

00:04:09.516 --> 00:04:12.452
asset in the world, and
so my dream is to buy

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:15.822
Tootsie Rolls, or K-Swiss
sneakers, or what have you,

00:04:15.822 --> 00:04:19.692
at scale, 50, 60, 100,
200 million dollars,

00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:22.629
do the thing that I do, 
and then flip it

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:24.097
for one billion dollars.

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:27.167
And that's how I think I,
from a self-aware standpoint,

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:29.903
can achieve my audacious goal.

00:04:29.903 --> 00:04:33.473
To do that, I knew that
I needed to understand

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:36.977
marketing and media at the
scale of which I wasn't

00:04:36.977 --> 00:04:39.780
playing at as an entrepreneur
and as an investor.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.583
So, I decided seven years
ago to eat shit for a decade

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:46.820
and build a client 
service business.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:48.955
(laughter)

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:50.290
(chuckles)

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:54.361
And I needed to learn why
all these big brands were

00:04:54.361 --> 00:04:56.563
doing the things that
I thought were stupid

00:04:56.563 --> 00:04:58.565
because I didn't have
the audacity or the ego

00:04:58.565 --> 00:05:00.333
to think they were stupid,
I just knew they were

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:01.501
playing a different game.

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:02.869
I had to understand why.

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:05.505
I'm a bad student, 
I learn by doing,

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:09.910
and so I set out to build
the best marketing machine,

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:13.914
is what I would call
it more than an agency.

00:05:13.914 --> 00:05:15.015
We've grown very fast.

00:05:15.015 --> 00:05:16.750
First two years I was still
busy with the wine business

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.018
and I had a book 
that went very viral

00:05:18.018 --> 00:05:21.087
that kind of dragged 
me out there.

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:22.422
But for the last five
years I've run the business

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:24.658
we've gone from 
30 to 750 people,

00:05:24.658 --> 00:05:26.693
from three to 
100 million in revenue,

00:05:26.693 --> 00:05:28.595
all organic, no M-and-A.

00:05:28.595 --> 00:05:30.463
So, we've built 
a real big business,

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:31.765
and I think we've 
done it because

00:05:31.765 --> 00:05:33.833
our interests have been
aligned with our clients

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:35.869
because I'm not trying 
to build an agency

00:05:35.869 --> 00:05:38.939
to sell to Martin Sorrell,
I'm trying to build

00:05:38.939 --> 00:05:42.909
an agency for myself 
to become my clients,

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:45.779
which has allowed us to be
very aligned with our clients,

00:05:45.779 --> 00:05:47.881
which is very different
than the marketplace

00:05:47.881 --> 00:05:50.550
of media agencies 
and marketing agencies.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.219
And we also do every capability

00:05:52.219 --> 00:05:53.753
because I'm building 
it for myself.

00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:55.422
We do media buying, 
and creative,

00:05:55.422 --> 00:05:57.057
and analytics, and dev.

00:05:57.057 --> 00:05:59.960
So, that's why it happened.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:01.194
- Well, let's talk about
the early days then.

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:03.430
You said you looked around
and saw all these brands

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.065
doing quote, unquote, 
stupid things.

00:06:05.065 --> 00:06:06.233
- Yes.

00:06:06.233 --> 00:06:07.801
- You don't have to 
name names if you

00:06:07.801 --> 00:06:08.635
don't want to.
- Television.

00:06:08.635 --> 00:06:10.136
- But give me 
some examples about

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:12.872
what were people doing wrong?

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:17.577
- To sit in this room in
October 2016 and have...

00:06:17.944 --> 00:06:19.779
You know, it's funny.

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:21.514
Go out with one of your
friends that has nothing

00:06:21.514 --> 00:06:23.183
to do with 
marketing or business,

00:06:23.183 --> 00:06:24.718
and explain the following:

00:06:24.718 --> 00:06:27.120
okay, so here's how it works.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.092
A human, a single human, at
an agency, a creative lead,

00:06:32.092 --> 00:06:34.694
and another human 
on the brand side.

00:06:34.694 --> 00:06:36.529
And let me tell you about
the human on the brand side.

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:39.966
This person went to business
school to sell stuff

00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:42.235
and run an operational P-and-L.

00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:46.439
These two people sit, 
the creative single person

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:50.410
humanly makes a 
subjective call on a tagline

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:53.346
and a video concept,
narrows it down from the

00:06:53.346 --> 00:06:56.683
seven or eight ideations
of his or her team

00:06:56.683 --> 00:06:59.586
to one or two, because you only
have an hour to present it.

00:06:59.586 --> 00:07:03.390
They go to the human that
is there to run a P-and-L.,

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:07.627
and the two of them make
a subjective creative call

00:07:07.627 --> 00:07:10.664
of what that business should
stand for for the entire year,

00:07:10.664 --> 00:07:15.535
and then that company takes
60 to 80% of it's monies

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:20.106
and makes a single 30
second video to be played

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:24.277
in a commercial format in 2016,

00:07:24.277 --> 00:07:28.114
and this is to drive the
sales of their business.

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:31.351
There's no common 
sensed human on Earth

00:07:31.351 --> 00:07:33.153
that thinks that's a good idea.

00:07:33.153 --> 00:07:34.054
(laughter)

00:07:34.054 --> 00:07:36.423
We in the marketing 
world play that game.

00:07:36.423 --> 00:07:39.259
I think that's insanity,
that excites me,

00:07:39.259 --> 00:07:41.227
that's the opportunity.

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:44.030
- Awesome.
(laughter)

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.432
- No, but really.

00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:49.502
I don't know who does
what, and I could be wrong.

00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:50.737
Let the market play itself out,

00:07:50.737 --> 00:07:55.275
but who watches 
a commercial in 2016?

00:07:56.276 --> 00:07:57.711
So, here, and I want 
to quantify this,

00:07:57.711 --> 00:07:59.779
I'm not a digitalist 
or a futurist,

00:07:59.779 --> 00:08:01.715
I think the 
Super Bowl commercial

00:08:01.715 --> 00:08:04.384
is the number one 
buy in marketing.

00:08:04.384 --> 00:08:06.019
If I buy a brand tomorrow,

00:08:06.019 --> 00:08:07.387
and I have a limited 
amount of money,

00:08:07.387 --> 00:08:09.322
the first thing 
I buy is the Super Bowl

00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:12.892
because everybody in America
is going to pay attention

00:08:12.892 --> 00:08:15.628
to that whether they watch it
on YouTube or during the game,

00:08:15.628 --> 00:08:18.098
and I have 30 seconds to
tell you what I'm about.

00:08:18.098 --> 00:08:21.134
The problem with Super Bowl
ads is they're a showcase

00:08:21.134 --> 00:08:23.570
for creative agencies 
to get new clients

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:25.739
and acquire more talent.

00:08:25.739 --> 00:08:28.541
They're not there to sell shit.

00:08:28.541 --> 00:08:30.910
So, I think that's the
best buy in marketing.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:33.246
I think the worst buy in
marketing in the world

00:08:33.246 --> 00:08:36.916
is the second most expensive
commercial on television.

00:08:36.916 --> 00:08:39.119
I don't know what that is,
whether it's the Oscar's,

00:08:39.119 --> 00:08:42.188
or the A.F.C. championship
game, or the Emmy's.

00:08:42.188 --> 00:08:44.224
I don't know what it
is, but it's the worst

00:08:44.224 --> 00:08:47.260
because we don't 
watch those commercials.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.262
It goes to commercial
and we grab our phone

00:08:49.262 --> 00:08:52.031
and we talk about that
dress, or that play,

00:08:52.031 --> 00:08:53.166
or what just happened.

00:08:53.166 --> 00:08:54.734
Our attention's not there.

00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:56.002
My living is very simple.

00:08:56.002 --> 00:08:58.471
In my bio where it says
I build businesses,

00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:01.641
I don't know what paper of my
team sends around these days,

00:09:01.641 --> 00:09:04.411
but on the website 
it says comma,

00:09:04.411 --> 00:09:06.479
"I day trade attention."

00:09:06.479 --> 00:09:08.648
Attention is the only asset.

00:09:08.648 --> 00:09:10.417
It's the only asset.

00:09:10.417 --> 00:09:12.952
And there's 
overpriced attention,

00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:14.854
and there's 
under-priced attention.

00:09:14.854 --> 00:09:17.023
Direct mail is 
overpriced attention.

00:09:17.023 --> 00:09:18.191
It works.

00:09:18.191 --> 00:09:19.959
Spend $100,000 
in direct mail,

00:09:19.959 --> 00:09:21.528
you will get things happen,

00:09:21.528 --> 00:09:23.797
but when you know that
there's a Facebook-targeted

00:09:23.797 --> 00:09:27.200
world out there that you could
do it better and cheaper,

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:28.968
that's a better idea.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.105
That's just what I spend
my whole life doing.

00:09:32.105 --> 00:09:35.341
What's overpriced, what's
under-priced, what's practical.

00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:37.410
I try to put myself out
of business everyday.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:39.179
If I started VaynerMedia today,

00:09:39.179 --> 00:09:42.248
we would be an Alexa 
voice message bot,

00:09:42.248 --> 00:09:44.651
top level domain consultant,

00:09:44.651 --> 00:09:48.288
video production 
vertical phone shop.

00:09:48.288 --> 00:09:49.823
(laughter)

00:09:49.823 --> 00:09:52.192
(chuckles)

00:09:52.192 --> 00:09:53.126
I don't know.

00:09:53.126 --> 00:09:55.462
What are we talking about?
(laughter)

00:09:55.462 --> 00:09:56.663
- Well I think that yeah,
we're, just hearing about

00:09:56.663 --> 00:09:58.998
what you guys are, I mean,
you talked about television,

00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:01.000
you talked about Facebook,
so it, that from,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.569
you know, I would assume
that people in the room here

00:10:02.569 --> 00:10:03.937
could say there 
are certain agencies

00:10:03.937 --> 00:10:05.038
that specialize in television,

00:10:05.038 --> 00:10:06.940
there are certain agencies
that specialize in digital.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:08.541
There are certain 
agencies that yeah,

00:10:08.541 --> 00:10:09.776
like you're saying, are specific

00:10:09.776 --> 00:10:12.679
voice recognition 
chat bots, you guys

00:10:12.679 --> 00:10:15.081
do actually wanna 
conquer it all.

00:10:15.081 --> 00:10:17.550
- Yes, but when 
I say conquer it all,

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:20.086
A, my first level of
responsibility

00:10:20.086 --> 00:10:22.856
is to deliver for our clients,
'cause they're paying us.

00:10:22.856 --> 00:10:25.425
So Vayner in 
it's current form today,

00:10:25.425 --> 00:10:27.060
what we wanna do for a living is

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.928
we wanna spend our clients money

00:10:28.928 --> 00:10:31.598
in a way that sells 
them the most stuff,

00:10:31.598 --> 00:10:33.233
or whatever they're 
trying to achieve,

00:10:33.233 --> 00:10:35.869
and we are completely
agnostic to where that is.

00:10:35.869 --> 00:10:37.837
I built my career on Twitter.

00:10:37.837 --> 00:10:39.839
It's where I came out of, 
for some of you

00:10:39.839 --> 00:10:40.874
that know who I am.

00:10:40.874 --> 00:10:44.344
I wasn't happy that Twitter
was losing attention in 2011,

00:10:44.344 --> 00:10:47.147
but when I was making those
videos five years ago,

00:10:47.147 --> 00:10:50.984
like, I'm worried about this,
like, I was worried about it.

00:10:50.984 --> 00:10:51.885
I was at the top of the heap,

00:10:51.885 --> 00:10:53.453
I was one of the 25 most
followed people

00:10:53.453 --> 00:10:55.822
in the world on Twitter,
like life was good.

00:10:55.822 --> 00:10:58.157
I wish Twitter stayed
for a thousand years,

00:10:58.157 --> 00:11:00.793
it would have been easy, 
I wasn't happy to put in work

00:11:00.793 --> 00:11:03.530
on Vine, and Snapchat 
and Instagram.

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:07.200
It's just, it's not my choice,
and what I'm fascinated by

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.236
is how many people are willing
to draw lines in the sand

00:11:10.236 --> 00:11:12.705
and hold on to things
because they're in their

00:11:12.705 --> 00:11:15.575
short-term financial 
vested interest.

00:11:15.575 --> 00:11:18.912
It is a massive business
vulnerability, and--

00:11:18.912 --> 00:11:20.246
- So what do people 
need to get rid of?

00:11:20.246 --> 00:11:22.015
Like if you had to give
people a list of like,

00:11:22.015 --> 00:11:23.149
three things maybe 
that people should

00:11:23.149 --> 00:11:24.684
stop doing in their 
current agencies.

00:11:24.684 --> 00:11:28.254
- Emotion, emotion's a
good thing to get rid of.

00:11:28.254 --> 00:11:29.255
And I mean that.

00:11:29.255 --> 00:11:31.124
I think that a lot 
of things that are

00:11:31.124 --> 00:11:33.593
holding people back 
is the emotion of it,

00:11:33.593 --> 00:11:36.095
they hold certain 
things on a pedestal.

00:11:36.095 --> 00:11:38.231
We hold, for example, let me,

00:11:38.231 --> 00:11:39.766
let me give you a good example.

00:11:39.766 --> 00:11:42.268
We think, somehow in 
our current society,

00:11:42.268 --> 00:11:44.470
we've decided that if you write

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:47.974
another human being a letter,
that you are somehow now

00:11:47.974 --> 00:11:49.776
the greatest fucking 
human being on Earth.

00:11:49.776 --> 00:11:50.843
(laughing)

00:11:50.843 --> 00:11:53.346
Like, we put things on
pedestals for no reason.

00:11:53.346 --> 00:11:56.783
We are so scared of change,
that we put writing a letter

00:11:56.783 --> 00:11:59.118
on a pedestal, 'cause this
somehow means

00:11:59.118 --> 00:12:02.622
you care so much, versus a text,

00:12:02.622 --> 00:12:05.692
which at the end of the day
are exactly the same thing

00:12:05.692 --> 00:12:07.460
if you write the 
same thing, it's,

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:10.196
the message is the same,
it's the medium of delivery.

00:12:10.196 --> 00:12:12.265
So I think the first thing
that people need to do

00:12:12.265 --> 00:12:14.934
is actually get rid of emotion.

00:12:14.934 --> 00:12:19.672
Number two, is have a real
conversation about measurement.

00:12:21.174 --> 00:12:22.809
Yeah.
(laughing)

00:12:22.809 --> 00:12:24.110
Like, measurement is--

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:26.512
You know, one of the great
things that happened to me

00:12:26.512 --> 00:12:30.350
is I started an agency knowing
nothing about the business.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:31.451
And I mean nothing.

00:12:31.451 --> 00:12:33.686
I knew nothing, it was
actually quite stupid.

00:12:33.686 --> 00:12:37.156
But you know, 
what I did know was

00:12:37.156 --> 00:12:39.492
my life as an immigrant, 
that had a family

00:12:39.492 --> 00:12:42.462
and liquor store business,
are KPI was not winning

00:12:42.462 --> 00:12:45.732
an award in France, or getting
a headline in a magazine,

00:12:45.732 --> 00:12:47.533
or some bullshit modeling mix

00:12:47.533 --> 00:12:49.769
that was manipulated 
to force the company

00:12:49.769 --> 00:12:52.639
to spend money on 
what they believe in.

00:12:52.639 --> 00:12:55.775
Like, our KPI was selling
Pinot Grigio, right?

00:12:55.775 --> 00:12:58.177
And so having that 
DNA has helped me.

00:12:58.177 --> 00:13:00.346
The amount of times I've
walked into a meeting

00:13:00.346 --> 00:13:01.981
where the 
Millard Brown case study,

00:13:01.981 --> 00:13:04.851
